-- title:
--   The symmetric power of a smooth affine curve is smooth: the local rings of $(B^{\otimes g})^{S_g}$ at maximal ideals are regular of dimension $g$
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field and let $B$ be a Dedekind domain which is a finitely generated $k$-algebra and not a field, so that $X=\operatorname{Spec}B$ is a smooth affine curve over $k$. For $g\ge0$ let $B^{\otimes g}=B\otimes_k\cdots\otimes_k B$ ($g$ factors; the coordinate ring of $X^g$), on which the symmetric group $S_g$ acts by permuting the tensor factors,
--   $$\sigma\cdot(b_1\otimes\cdots\otimes b_g)=b_{\sigma^{-1}(1)}\otimes\cdots\otimes b_{\sigma^{-1}(g)},$$
--   and let $S=(B^{\otimes g})^{S_g}$ be the $k$-subalgebra of invariants, the coordinate ring of the symmetric power $\operatorname{Sym}^gX=X^g/S_g$.
--
--   Then for every maximal ideal $\mathfrak m$ of $S$ the localization $S_{\mathfrak m}$ is a regular local ring of Krull dimension $g$. In other words, $\operatorname{Sym}^gX$ is smooth of dimension $g$ over $k$ at every closed point, although the quotient map $X^g\to\operatorname{Sym}^gX$ is ramified along the diagonals.
--
--   Role: this is the local input for the construction of the Jacobian of a curve as a quotient of $\operatorname{Sym}^gC$ (Weil, Chow, Milne): the charts $\operatorname{Sym}^g(\operatorname{Spec}B)$ of $\operatorname{Sym}^gC$ are regular. For $B=k[x]$ the ring $S$ is the polynomial ring in the elementary symmetric functions; in general the maximal ideal of $S_{\mathfrak m}$ is generated by $g$ elements, the elementary symmetric functions in an étale coordinate centred at the points of $\mathfrak m$.
--
--   **Formalization Note**: $B^{\otimes g}$ is Mathlib's `PiTensorProduct k (fun _ : Fin g => B)` and the action of $\sigma\in S_g$ = `Equiv.Perm (Fin g)` is `PiTensorProduct.reindex k (fun _ : Fin g => B) σ`, which sends `tprod k b` to `tprod k (fun i => b (σ.symm i))`. The invariant subalgebra is the variable `S : Subalgebra k (PiTensorProduct k (fun _ : Fin g => B))`, fixed uniquely by its membership condition `hS` (an element lies in `S` iff it is fixed by every `σ`). $\mathfrak m$ is `M : Ideal S` with `[M.IsMaximal]`, $S_{\mathfrak m}$ is `Localization.AtPrime M`, regularity is Mathlib's `IsRegularLocalRing` and the dimension is `ringKrullDim`. The hypotheses are satisfiable and the conclusion is not vacuous: for example $B=k[x]$ (`Polynomial k`), any $g$, and $S$ the invariant subalgebra; there $S$ is a polynomial ring in $g$ variables and the statement says its local rings at maximal ideals are regular of dimension $g$.
-- source:
--   J. S. Milne, Jacobian Varieties, in: G. Cornell, J. H. Silverman (eds.), Arithmetic Geometry, Springer (1986), §3 (symmetric powers of a curve); A. Weil, Variétés abéliennes et courbes algébriques, Hermann (1948); D. Mumford, Abelian Varieties, Oxford University Press (1970), §3

import Mathlib

set_option autoImplicit false

theorem IsDedekindDomain.isRegularLocalRing_localization_symmetricPower
    {k : Type*} [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k] {B : Type*} [CommRing B] [IsDedekindDomain B] [Algebra k B]
    [Algebra.FiniteType k B] (hB : ¬ IsField B) (g : ℕ)
    (S : Subalgebra k (PiTensorProduct k (fun _ : Fin g => B)))
    (hS : ∀ x, x ∈ S ↔ ∀ σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin g), PiTensorProduct.reindex k (fun _ : Fin g => B) σ x = x)
    (M : Ideal S) [M.IsMaximal] :
    IsRegularLocalRing (Localization.AtPrime M) ∧ ringKrullDim (Localization.AtPrime M) = g := by
